-- title:
--   §4.2.2 — per-period log loss on extreme realizations, $2[-\ln(1-n\tau)-n\tau\ln(n\tau)]-2n\tau\ln\frac12$
-- statement:
--   Let $(X,\nu)$ be a probability space carrying a state $\omega\in\{0,1\}$ and, for each expert $i\in[n]$, an information $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal G_i$ and a forecast $F_i$ that is $\mathcal G_i$-measurable and a version of $\mathbb P(\omega=1\mid\mathcal G_i)$, i.e. $\nu(\{\omega=1\}\cap E)=\int_E F_i\,d\nu$ for every $E\in\mathcal G_i$. This is an arbitrary information structure $I$ (not necessarily a partial evidence one): in the paper $X=\Omega\times S_1\times\dots\times S_n$, $\mathcal G_i$ is generated by $s_i$ and $F_i=I(\omega=1,s_i)/(I(\omega=1,s_i)+I(\omega=0,s_i))$. For $\tau>0$ let
--
--   $$
--   \zeta_0(\tau)=\{\exists i:\ F_i\le\tau\},\qquad \zeta_1(\tau)=\{\exists i:\ F_i\ge1-\tau\}
--   $$
--
--   be the events that some expert's forecast is extreme low, resp. extreme high.
--
--   Let $n\tau<1$. On extreme realizations the algorithm forecasts
--
--   $$
--   r=\begin{cases} n\tau & \text{on }\zeta_0(\tau)\setminus\zeta_1(\tau),\\ 1-n\tau & \text{on }\zeta_1(\tau)\setminus\zeta_0(\tau),\\ \tfrac12 & \text{on }\zeta_0(\tau)\cap\zeta_1(\tau).\end{cases}
--   $$
--
--   Then its expected logarithmic loss on the extreme event is bounded:
--
--   $$
--   \int_{\zeta_0(\tau)\cup\zeta_1(\tau)} l(r,\omega)\,d\nu\le 2\bigl[-1\cdot\ln(1-n\tau)-n\tau\ln(n\tau)\bigr]-2n\tau\ln\tfrac12 ,
--   $$
--
--   and the right side is $O\bigl(n\tau\ln\frac1{n\tau}\bigr)$: for some constant $C$, $2[-\ln(1-x)-x\ln x]-2x\ln\frac12\le C\,x\ln\frac1x$ for all $x\in(0,\frac12]$.
--
--   With $\tau=T^{-1/2}$, summing over $T$ periods gives Claim (1) of the proof of Theorem 2: total regret $\tilde O(n\sqrt T)$ on extreme periods.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The expected loss on the extreme event is a lower Lebesgue integral of the $[0,\infty]$-valued loss.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: information structure I ∈ Δ(Ω × S_1 × … × S_n) with F_i = I(ω = 1, s_i)/(I(ω = 1, s_i) + I(ω = 0, s_i)) → any probability space with forecasts F_i that are 𝓖_i-measurable versions of P(ω = 1 | 𝓖_i) for sub-σ-algebras 𝓖_i; reason: the paper's structure is the case 𝓖_i = σ(s_i)
--   - generalization: τ = T^{−1/2} → every τ > 0 with nτ < 1; reason: the paper's τ is a case (for T > n²)
--   - reading: ζ_1(τ) \ ζ_0(τ): "an extreme forecast in [1 − τ, 1] and no extreme forecast in [1 − τ, 1]"; ζ_1 as "[1, 1 − α]" → ζ_1(τ) = {∃ i, F_i ≥ 1 − τ} and no forecast in [0, τ]; reason: the printed parenthetical contradicts itself; ζ_1(τ) \ ζ_0(τ) means no forecast in [0, τ]
--   - reading: "bound on the logarithmic loss in each period" → the expected log loss of the rule over the extreme event ζ_0(τ) ∪ ζ_1(τ); reason: the bound is derived from Lemmas 2–4, which are statements about probabilities, so it is an expectation
--   - generalization: = O(nτ ln(1/(nτ))) → ≤ C · x ln(1/x) for all x = nτ ∈ (0, 1/2], some C; reason: a bound on (0, 1/2] implies the O-bound as nτ → 0
-- source:
--   Babichenko, Garber, Learning of Optimal Forecast Aggregation in Partial Evidence Environments, arXiv:1802.07107v1, §4.2.2, display and rule, pp.20–21; Claim (1), p.17

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ForecastAggLearn_logLoss

namespace ForecastAggLearn

open MeasureTheory

/-- **Per-period loss bound on extreme realizations.**
Babichenko, Garber, arXiv:1802.07107v1, §4.2.2, display, p.21 (with the rule of pp.20–21): on
`ζ_0(τ) \ ζ_1(τ)` the algorithm forecasts `r = nτ`, on `ζ_1(τ) \ ζ_0(τ)` it forecasts `r = 1 − nτ`,
on `ζ_0(τ) ∩ ζ_1(τ)` it forecasts `r = 1/2`, and "The bounds of Lemmas 2,3 and 4 yield the
following bound on the logarithmic loss in each period:
2[−1 · ln(1 − nτ) − nτ ln(nτ)] − 2nτ ln(1/2) = O(nτ ln(1/(nτ)))".

The expected log loss of this rule on the extreme event `ζ_0(τ) ∪ ζ_1(τ)` is at most
`2[−ln(1 − nτ) − nτ ln(nτ)] − 2nτ ln(1/2)`, and this bound is `O(x ln(1/x))` in `x = nτ`: it is at
most `C · x ln(1/x)` for every `x ∈ (0, 1/2]`, for some constant `C`.

**Formalization Note.** The setting is that of `lemma_2` (an arbitrary information structure with
forecasts that are versions of `P(ω = 1 | 𝓖 i)`). The expected loss on the extreme event is
`∫⁻_{ζ_0 ∪ ζ_1} l(r, ω) dν` in `ℝ≥0∞`. Stated for every `τ > 0` with `nτ < 1` (so the forecasts are
in `(0, 1)`); the paper takes `τ = T^{−1/2}`. The `O(·)` as `nτ → 0` is stated as a bound on
`(0, 1/2]`, which implies it. On p.20 the parenthetical "no extreme forecast in
[1 − τ, 1]" for `ζ_1(τ) \ ζ_0(τ)` is read as "no extreme forecast in [0, τ]". -/
theorem extreme_period_bound {X : Type*} [mX : MeasurableSpace X] (ν : Measure X)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (ω : X → Bool) (hω : Measurable ω) {n : ℕ} (𝓖 : Fin n → MeasurableSpace X)
    (h𝓖 : ∀ i, 𝓖 i ≤ mX) (F : Fin n → X → ℝ) (hFm : ∀ i, Measurable[𝓖 i] (F i))
    (hF : ∀ i, ∀ E, MeasurableSet[𝓖 i] E →
      ν ({x | ω x = true} ∩ E) = ∫⁻ x in E, ENNReal.ofReal (F i x) ∂ν)
    (τ : ℝ) (hτ : 0 < τ) (hnτ : n * τ < 1) :
    (let ζ0 : Set X := {x | ∃ i, F i x ≤ τ}
     let ζ1 : Set X := {x | ∃ i, 1 - τ ≤ F i x}
     let r : X → ℝ := fun x =>
       if x ∈ ζ0 ∧ x ∈ ζ1 then 1 / 2 else if x ∈ ζ0 then n * τ else 1 - n * τ
     ∫⁻ x in ζ0 ∪ ζ1, logLoss (r x) (ω x) ∂ν ≤
       ENNReal.ofReal (2 * (-1 * Real.log (1 - n * τ) - n * τ * Real.log (n * τ)) -
         2 * n * τ * Real.log (1 / 2))) ∧
    ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ x : ℝ, 0 < x → x ≤ 1 / 2 →
      2 * (-1 * Real.log (1 - x) - x * Real.log x) - 2 * x * Real.log (1 / 2) ≤
        C * (x * Real.log (1 / x)) := by sorry

end ForecastAggLearn
